<div class="article-body">

<p>Aim for an EC of 1.2 to 1.8 mS/cm for lettuce, 1.0 to 1.6 for basil and herbs, 1.8 to 2.3 for spinach, and 2.0 to 4.0 for tomatoes and peppers. That is the whole hydroponic EC answer on one line. The reason it still confuses people is that almost no beginner meter shows you EC. It shows a PPM number built on a multiplier the manufacturer never prints on the box.</p>

<p>My first meter read 1920. I had no idea whether that was perfect or lethal, so I did what most beginners do. I guessed. I dumped six gallons of nutrient solution down the kitchen sink in one month, and my lettuce stalled anyway.</p>

<p>Here is what I did not know: 1920 was not parts per million. It was microsiemens per centimeter, which is an EC of 1.92. My lettuce wanted 1.2 to 1.8. I was closer to right than I had been in weeks, and I threw away a working reservoir because I could not read the units on my own pen.</p>

<h2 id="what">1. What EC Actually Measures (and What It Doesn't)</h2>

<p>EC stands for electrical conductivity. It works because of one property of dissolved salts: once they dissolve, they split into charged particles that carry current. Pure water barely conducts anything. Add nutrient salts and the water starts conducting, and it conducts more as you add more.</p>

<p>So an EC reading answers exactly one question. How much dissolved salt is in this water? Nothing else. It does not tell you which salts, in what ratio, or whether the plant can absorb them. Remember that, because it becomes the twist later in this article.</p>

<p>The tool itself is almost comically simple. A cheap pen has two metal electrodes in the tip. It pushes a small current between them and measures how easily the current passes. No chemistry, no optics, no film. This is also why a dead-looking reading is usually not a dead meter. A pen dropped into distilled or reverse osmosis water reads 0.0 or bounces between 0.01 and 0.05. Nothing is broken. There is simply nothing in the water to conduct.</p>

<h2 id="hidden">2. Why Your Meter Says PPM When You Asked for EC</h2>

<p>Here is the part that took me six weeks to find out. A TDS meter does not measure TDS. There is no sensor inside a $15 pen that can count dissolved solids. Every TDS pen on the market measures conductivity and then multiplies that number by a conversion factor baked into the firmware.</p>

<p>The manufacturer picks the factor. The box usually does not tell you which one.</p>

<p>There are three common factors in circulation. The 500 scale comes from a sodium chloride reference. The 700 scale comes from a potassium chloride reference. A third, the 442 scale, sits at 640 and shows up in some water testing gear. Feed the same reservoir to three pens and you get three different "PPM" numbers, all technically correct.</p>

