<p><strong>Here is the key insight:</strong> the same reservoir is 700 ppm on my pen and 980 ppm on a pen that costs the same money. That 40% gap is not drift and not a bad unit. It is two different guesses about what your salts are made of. Which means every forum post, every YouTube video, and every nutrient bottle that tells you "run 900 ppm" is giving you a number without the unit that makes it usable.</p>

<p>That is how two people can argue for an hour about whether 900 ppm is too hot for lettuce and both be right. One of them is talking about EC 1.3 and the other about EC 1.8.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">The 60-second fix for your pen</div>
  <p>Stir a batch of nutrient water, take a reading, then divide the PPM number by your target EC. If you read 700 ppm on water you know is EC 1.4, your factor is 500. If you read 980, it is 700. Write that number on a piece of tape and stick it to the pen. Problem solved for good.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="starve">3. The Perfect EC That Still Starves Your Plants</h2>

<p>I thought I had the unit thing figured out. Then my lettuce yellowed at a textbook-perfect 1.6 mS/cm.</p>

<p>Not slightly faded. Old leaves going pale between the veins, new growth smaller than the last set, the whole tray slowing down. My EC was right. My nutrients were fresh. I had measured everything the internet told me to measure, twice.</p>

<p><strong>This is where it gets interesting.</strong> I checked pH for the first time in two weeks. It was 7.4.</p>

<p>Here is why that mattered. EC tells you how much salt is dissolved in the water. It says nothing about whether the roots can take it. Plants do not absorb iron, manganese, zinc, or boron as raw salt. Those elements have to be dissolved in a form the root membrane can pull across, and that form only exists in a narrow pH window, roughly 5.5 to 6.5 for most hydroponic crops. Above that, iron and manganese drop out of solution and land on the roots as a coating the plant cannot use.</p>

<p>So my reservoir was full of the exact nutrients my lettuce needed, present in the exact concentration my meter confirmed, sitting an arm's length from the roots, and unavailable. I was feeding a plant with a locked pantry door.</p>

<p>Let me be clear about the trap here, because it is the expensive one. Every guide that tells you to chase a PPM number trains you to look at the wrong dial. You can hit a perfect EC and kill a plant with a pH you never tested. The number that measures quantity cannot tell you anything about availability.</p>

<p>If the leaves are yellowing, start with <a href="/posts/hydroponic-leaves-turning-yellow">the deficiency chart I use</a> before you touch your nutrient bottle. Nine times out of ten it is pH or a stale reservoir, not a missing nutrient.</p>

<h2 id="baseline">4. The Two Numbers Worth Writing on Tape</h2>

<p>You only need two readings to stop guessing. Both take a minute, and neither one costs money after the first test.</p>

<p><strong>Number one is your source water.</strong> Test your plain tap water before you mix anything into it. If your tap reads EC 0.4, that 0.4 is baked into every reading you will ever take. Your lettuce target of 1.4 means 1.4 total on the meter, so you need to add nutrients worth 1.0 and stop. That tap water also carries calcium, magnesium and carbonate, and you paid for none of it. This is the number people skip, and it is the reason some growers swear their plants burn at 1.2 while someone else runs 2.0 without a mark.</p>

<p>Hard water makes this worse. If your tap is above 0.6 or your utility uses chloramine, water quality problems will follow you into every reservoir. My full <a href="/posts/hydroponic-nutrient-guide">nutrient guide</a> covers the mixing order and what to do about source water that fights you.</p>

<p><strong>Number two is the direction your reading moves.</strong> One reading is a snapshot. Two readings a few days apart are a diagnosis. Pay attention to this number and the reservoir starts telling you what to do.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>EC rising while the water level drops:</strong> the plant is drinking more water than salt. The solution is getting stronger on its own. Dilute with plain water.</li>
  <li><strong>EC falling while the water level drops:</strong> the plant is eating faster than it drinks. Top up with nutrient water at a stronger mix.</li>
  <li><strong>EC roughly flat as the water drops:</strong> the ratio is right. Just replace the volume with weak nutrient water and leave the strength alone.</li>
</ul>

<p>That third case is the one worth chasing. When the reading holds steady, the plant is taking water and nutrients in the proportion you mixed them, which means the recipe fits the crop. This is also why I now handle my top-offs by volume instead of by calendar. My <a href="/posts/how-often-to-change-hydroponic-water">reservoir change schedule</a> covers the one-third rule that replaced my Sunday ritual.</p>

