<p>Three things in that table are worth more than the numbers themselves.</p>

<p><strong>Seedlings want a quarter of what you think.</strong> A full-strength reservoir drowns a two-week-old plant. Start at 0.4 and walk it up. If you are running a jar of basil on a shelf, that chart is the difference between a plant that stalls and a plant that never notices.</p>

<p><strong>Microgreens need zero nutrients.</strong> They live on the energy stored in the seed for the entire 10 to 14 days they are on your shelf. If you are adding anything to their water, you are buying nutrients for a plant that cannot drink them yet. My <a href="/posts/how-to-grow-microgreens-indoors">microgreens guide</a> covers the water-only routine.</p>

<p><strong>Fruiting crops are a different sport.</strong> A tomato or a pepper at 2.0 to 4.0 mS/cm will burn a lettuce grown in the same bucket. If you run one reservoir, run it at lettuce strength and accept slower fruit, or split into two reservoirs. My <a href="/posts/how-to-grow-peppers-indoors">indoor peppers guide</a> is where that decision actually starts to matter.</p>

<h2 id="measure">6. How to Take a Reading That Means Something</h2>

<p>Five things decide whether the number on the screen is worth anything.</p>

<p><strong>Calibrate first.</strong> A pen that has never been calibrated is a random number generator with a screen. Buy a bottle of 1413 µS/cm reference solution, which is EC 1.413, and dial the pen to it. Once a month is enough for a home setup, more often if you use it daily.</p>

<p><strong>Stir, then wait.</strong> Readings need 30 to 60 seconds to stabilize, and they will not stabilize at all if you just poured in nutrients and did not mix. Undissolved salt sitting on the bottom is invisible to the probe.</p>

<p><strong>Watch the water temperature.</strong> Conductivity rises with heat, about 2% per degree Celsius. Water drawn cold from the tap reads low, and a reservoir sitting under a grow light reads high. Good meters compensate for this automatically. Cheap pens usually do not, so measure at the same point in the day to compare fairly.</p>

<p><strong>Rinse between readings.</strong> Dip the probe in distilled water and shake it dry. A drop of last week's concentrated solution in the cap is enough to skew the next reading.</p>

<p><strong>Store the probe wet.</strong> A dry electrode drifts and eventually dies. Most budget pens come with a cap you are meant to keep a little water in. Mine came with nothing, which is why my second pen lasted longer than my first.</p>

<h2 id="choose">7. Three Ways to Handle This (and What I Use)</h2>

<p>You do not need a lab. There are three honest levels here, and the middle one is where almost everyone should land.</p>

<p><strong>Option A: skip the meters and run fresh.</strong> Mix a quarter-strength solution, change it on a schedule, and never look at a number. It works, it is free, and it is what a <a href="/posts/kratky-method-for-beginners">Kratky jar</a> is built for. The cost is that you are flying blind on pH, which is the thing that actually broke my grow.</p>

<p><strong>Option B: a pH pen and an EC pen.</strong> This is what I use. Two small tools that fit in a drawer, and together they tell you the only two things a home reservoir needs to know. If you are buying one, buy the pH pen first, since a locked-out nutrient at perfect EC is the failure that costs you a crop.</p>

<p><strong>Option C: a controller that doses for you.</strong> A few hundred dollars, logs everything, corrects drift while you are at work. Genuinely useful if you travel or run multiple reservoirs. Overkill for four heads of lettuce, and one more pump that can fail while you are away.</p>

<p>The gear I actually keep on the shelf: a budget <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=EC+TDS+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">EC and TDS meter</a> for strength, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=digital+pH+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">digital pH meter</a> for availability, and a bottle of <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=EC+calibration+solution+1413+us+cm&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">1413 µS/cm calibration solution</a> so both of them keep telling the truth. The three together cost less than a single bottle of premium nutrients, and they last years.</p>

<p>For the reservoir itself, a basic <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroponic+nutrient+kit+masterblend&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">hydroponic nutrient kit</a> is all you need to hit any row in that chart. Skip the additives until the basics hold steady for a full grow.</p>

<p>And if your readings keep drifting no matter what you do, check the medium before you blame the water. Rockwool fresh out of the bag runs alkaline and can push your reservoir up for the first week, which I ran into during my <a href="/posts/hydroponic-growing-medium">growing medium test</a>. The fix is a pre-soak, not more pH down.</p>

<h2 id="honest">8. Here Is the Honest Truth</h2>

<p><strong>Here is the honest truth</strong> about the EC pen: a budget one drifts, has no temperature compensation, and goes blind if you let the electrode dry out. Mine has been wrong by a full 0.3 mS/cm more than once, which is the entire difference between lettuce strength and spinach strength. Calibration fixes it for ninety seconds of work, but the pen will lie to you in the meantime and you will not know.</p>

<p>The bigger honest truth is that if you are growing lettuce or herbs in a Kratky jar on a windowsill, you can skip the EC meter entirely. Buy the pH pen, keep it calibrated, and spend the difference on seeds. Concentration matters most for fruiting crops and for anyone running a reservoir large enough to drift. That is where the meter earns its place on the shelf.</p>

<p>If you will not calibrate anything, get <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pH+test+drops+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">pH test drops</a> instead. They cannot drift, they cost the price of a coffee, and a color you can actually read beats a precise number you cannot trust.</p>

<p>Here is the part that matters. The unit on the screen was never the problem. The problem was that I trusted a single number to tell me everything, when the reservoir was sending me three. Quantity, availability, and direction. Get those three and you stop guessing, whatever scale your pen happens to use.</p>

<p><strong>Your next move:</strong> test your tap water today and write that number on a piece of tape. Then take one reading, wait three days, read it again, and look at which way it moved. That is a real diagnosis, and it costs nothing. If you need a pen to start, the <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=EC+TDS+meter+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">EC and TDS meter</a> I use was $16 when I last checked and it bounces between $13 and $20. Anything under $20 is a solid deal, and it comes with the calibration bottle most listings leave out.</p>

